<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <!-- THE PROMISE: nothing about a person leaves this device. Not a name, not a temperature, not a
         symptom, not a dose, not an illness. Health used to keep that by declaring no permissions at
         all — but the permission count was the means, never the promise, and the promise is the part
         that must not move.

         So the test for anything added here is not "does it touch the network". It is: COULD THIS
         REQUEST TELL ANYONE SOMETHING ABOUT A MEMBER OF THIS HOUSEHOLD?

             "What is acetaminophen oral suspension, and what does its label say?"
                 → a question about a PRODUCT. Anyone could type it into a search engine.
             "This person takes these medicines"
                 → a question about a PERSON. It never leaves.

         INTERNET — two features ask the first kind of question, and neither can ask the second.

           * `data/net/DrugLookupClient` — the medicine cabinet's drug lookup. Only ever called
             because somebody pressed Search or Refresh; its only inputs are a search term and an
             RxNorm concept id. There is no parameter, and no code path, by which a profile, a
             reading or a dose could reach it. It asks two public, keyless U.S. government
             references — RxNorm (National Library of Medicine) and openFDA — and caches the answer
             locally so the same question isn't asked twice.

           * `data/net/ProviderDirectoryClient` — the Care tab's network check. It asks an insurer's
             own published provider directory whether it lists a clinician. Since the CMS
             Interoperability and Patient Access rule those directories are public, unauthenticated
             FHIR APIs (Da Vinci Plan-Net) — no key, no sign-in, no member number, by design —
             published precisely so anybody can look a doctor up before booking.

             Exactly three things can leave: the payer's own directory address, a practitioner's
             SURNAME, and a practitioner's NPI. The NPI is the national identifier printed on
             prescriptions and published in the federal NPPES registry. All three are facts about a
             URL or about a doctor.

                 "Does this public directory list Dr Okafor?"
                     → a question about a PRACTITIONER, which the directory exists to answer.
                 "Dr Okafor is my daughter's paediatrician"
                     → a question about a PERSON. It never leaves.

             The member id in particular is never sent, and cannot be: `findPractitioner` takes a
             base URL, a name and an NPI, and holds no reference to a plan, a membership or a
             profile. The insurance card itself is stored, rendered and exported entirely on-device.

         Neither client runs on a timer, at startup, or in the background. A household that never
         opens the search and never presses Check makes no request at all.

         POST_NOTIFICATIONS — medication reminders the user sets up per medicine, delivered by
         WorkManager (see `reminder/`). Composed and delivered entirely on-device. Nothing is
         scheduled until a reminder is turned on. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS" />

    <!-- HEALTH CONNECT — read-only, and the answer to the test above is that none of it leaves.

         Health Connect is the phone's own health store: Android 14 and later keep it in the
         platform, and the watch, scale and running apps a household already uses write into it.
         Health reads what it holds about the household's PRIMARY USER — the one person this phone
         belongs to, chosen in Health — into health.db, where it sits beside everything typed by
         hand. It is read on this device, kept on this device, and backed up only where the rest of
         Health's records are. Health writes nothing to Health Connect and sends nothing anywhere.

         Every data type is listed because Health imports every data type; Health Connect's own
         screen is where the household picks which of them to allow, and a type left off is simply
         not read. `connect/ConnectTypes` is the code's copy of this list; ConnectTypesTest checks the
         two agree. HISTORY lifts the thirty-day limit on the first import; IN_BACKGROUND lets the
         scheduled import run while Health is closed. -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.health.READ_ACTIVE_CALORIES_BURNED" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.health.READ_BASAL_BODY_TEMPERATURE" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.health.READ_BASAL_METABOLIC_RATE" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.health.READ_BLOOD_GLUCOSE" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.health.READ_BLOOD_PRESSURE" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.health.READ_BODY_FAT" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.health.READ_BODY_TEMPERATURE" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.health.READ_BODY_WATER_MASS" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.health.READ_BONE_MASS" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.health.READ_CERVICAL_MUCUS" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.health.READ_DISTANCE" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.health.READ_ELEVATION_GAINED" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.health.READ_EXERCISE" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.health.READ_FLOORS_CLIMBED" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.health.READ_HEALTH_DATA_HISTORY" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.health.READ_HEALTH_DATA_IN_BACKGROUND" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.health.READ_HEART_RATE" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.health.READ_HEART_RATE_VARIABILITY" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.health.READ_HEIGHT" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.health.READ_HYDRATION" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.health.READ_INTERMENSTRUAL_BLEEDING" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.health.READ_LEAN_BODY_MASS" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.health.READ_MEDICAL_DATA_ALLERGIES_INTOLERANCES" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.health.READ_MEDICAL_DATA_CONDITIONS" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.health.READ_MEDICAL_DATA_LABORATORY_RESULTS" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.health.READ_MEDICAL_DATA_MEDICATIONS" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.health.READ_MEDICAL_DATA_PERSONAL_DETAILS" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.health.READ_MEDICAL_DATA_PRACTITIONER_DETAILS" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.health.READ_MEDICAL_DATA_PREGNANCY" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.health.READ_MEDICAL_DATA_PROCEDURES" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.health.READ_MEDICAL_DATA_SOCIAL_HISTORY" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.health.READ_MEDICAL_DATA_VACCINES" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.health.READ_MEDICAL_DATA_VISITS" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.health.READ_MEDICAL_DATA_VITAL_SIGNS" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.health.READ_MENSTRUATION" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.health.READ_MINDFULNESS" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.health.READ_NUTRITION" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.health.READ_OVULATION_TEST" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.health.READ_OXYGEN_SATURATION" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.health.READ_PLANNED_EXERCISE" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.health.READ_POWER" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.health.READ_RESPIRATORY_RATE" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.health.READ_RESTING_HEART_RATE" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.health.READ_SEXUAL_ACTIVITY" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.health.READ_SKIN_TEMPERATURE" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.health.READ_SLEEP" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.health.READ_SPEED" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.health.READ_STEPS" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.health.READ_TOTAL_CALORIES_BURNED" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.health.READ_VO2_MAX" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.health.READ_WEIGHT" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.health.READ_WHEELCHAIR_PUSHES" />

    <!-- Health is a library module: no app-level attributes here (name/icon/theme/label live on the
         :app host). Only Health's own components are declared for the manifest merger. -->
    <application>

        <!-- Not a launcher: the Operations Sandbox home opens this explicitly. The theme is set on
             the activity so Health keeps its own launch look. -->
        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:theme="@style/Theme.Health"
            android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize" />

        <!-- What Health does with Health Connect data, in words. Health Connect links to it from the
             permission screen, and will not show that screen to an app that has no such page: on
             Android 14 and later it finds it through the alias below, which only the platform can
             start (START_VIEW_PERMISSION_USAGE is a signature permission). -->
        <activity
            android:name=".connect.HealthConnectPrivacyActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:theme="@style/Theme.Health">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="androidx.health.ACTION_SHOW_PERMISSIONS_RATIONALE" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>

        <activity-alias
            android:name=".connect.ViewPermissionUsageActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:permission="android.permission.START_VIEW_PERMISSION_USAGE"
            android:targetActivity=".connect.HealthConnectPrivacyActivity">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.VIEW_PERMISSION_USAGE" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.HEALTH_PERMISSIONS" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity-alias>

        <!-- Hands the exported insurance-card PDF to a share sheet, and nothing else: the paths file
             exposes cacheDir/exports only. The card PHOTOGRAPHS live in filesDir/insurance-cards and
             are deliberately not reachable through this provider — they are a record, not an export.

             A distinctly-named subclass because the manifest merger keys providers by class name:
             Health, Citation and LifeOps each need their own, or the merge collides. -->
        <provider
            android:name=".HealthFileProvider"
            android:authorities="${applicationId}.health.fileprovider"
            android:exported="false"
            android:grantUriPermissions="true">
            <meta-data
                android:name="android.support.FILE_PROVIDER_PATHS"
                android:resource="@xml/health_file_paths" />
        </provider>

    </application>

</manifest>
